{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Take the staff, etc. and He was sanctified through them.</b> This pericah — all the commentators of the Torah have been prolific upon it, and I too will seek to illuminate it in the light of Or HaChaim. And before we take our stand on the plain sense of the verses, let us preface by understanding what was the error of Moshe in this matter that was the cause of the decree upon him. And I have found that ten approaches were stated concerning the matter in the words of the commentators of the Torah, and they are these in brief:" "1) Rashi, zatzal, explained that the error of Moshe was that HaShem said to them: speak, and he struck. 2) Rabbi Avraham ibn Ezra explained that the error was that he caused it that the rock did not give its waters until the second strike, in that he lost focus on the first strike because of the contention of the assembly. 3) He [ibn Ezra?] brought the opinion of the aforementioned, that HaShem's displeasure was because he struck twice — for had he struck once there would have been no displeasure, since speaking to the rock is the striking. 4) He brought the opinion of the aforementioned, that HaShem's displeasure was because they did not recite a song over the waters, like ״עֲלִי בְאֵר״. 5) He brought the opinion of the aforementioned, that HaShem's displeasure was because he said to the children of the living El ״הַמּוֹרִים״, and it is not fitting for a pious man to despise and belittle the children of Avraham, Yitzchak, and Yaakov, etc. 6) The Rambam (in Shemonah Perakim) explained that HaShem was displeased with him because he became angered at the assembly, and for this reason they thought that HaShem too was angry with them — which was not so. 7) Rav Chananel, and the Ramban agreed with him, that HaShem's displeasure was because he said ״we will bring forth for you water״ and did not say ״He will bring forth for you water״ — for from this Israel erred and thought that by their own wisdom they performed the deed, for they had no indication that HaShem is the One who performs the miracle. Therefore HaShem was displeased and said ״you did not believe in Me, to sanctify Me״ etc. 8) Rabbi Moshe HaKohen explained that HaShem's displeasure was over Moshe's saying ״from this rock״ etc., and Moshe's intent was by virtue of his ability, if not with the supernal power, and the people thought that HaShem was unable to bring forth water from the rock — and this is the error of Moshe. And he brought proof for his interpretation from what the verse states (Psalms 106:33): ״כִּי הִמְרוּ אֶת רוּחוֹ וַיְבַטֵּא בִּשְׂפָתָיו״ etc. 9) The Rav Yosef Albo in Sefer HaIkkarim explains that HaShem's displeasure was because Moshe and Aharon did not bestir themselves of their own initiative to bring forth water for them and were hesitant, which indicates a deficiency in trust, etc. 10) Baal Maasei HaShem [i.e., Abarbanel] explained that there was a dispute between Moshe and Israel: Israel wanted to bring forth water for themselves from another place that they had dug, and Moshe did not wish to speak to that rock which they had dug. Moshe became angry and threw the staff from his hand — not to strike but in anger — and by happenstance the staff struck the rock, etc. See his words there." "<b>Here,</b> all ten approaches do not free us from the obligation of truth. Go forth and learn what the Ramban objected to three of them and rejected them, and what Rabbi Avraham ibn Ezra objected to three others and rejected them. And I will raise objections against the rest, namely: the one approach of Rav Chananel that the Ramban chose for himself — that the displeasure was because he ascribed the deed to himself and said ״we will bring forth״ etc. — was not Moshe known as the emissary of the Place [i.e., HaShem], and all his deeds are by the power of El? And even if it were his own power, is not the greatness of the Master known through the greatness of the servant many times over? Further, you will find in the pericah of Bo el Pharaoh that the verse states (Exodus 12:21): ״וַיִּקְרָא מֹשֶׁה לְכָל יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיֹּאמֶר אֲלֵהֶם מִשְׁכוּ וּקְחוּ״ etc., and he did not say it in the name of HaShem — for surely the matter is well known, that he is His emissary. Further, in the case before us, he was compelled to say ״we will bring forth for you,״ for they are the ones who bring it forth by their speech or by their striking, since HaShem had placed the bringing forth in their hand. As for the approach of the RI Albo, my mind does not assent to it, for after Moshe saw that HaShem had withheld water from them and the Well had departed, how could he step forward to bring forth water until he knew the mind of the Creator, blessed be He? And who would say that HaShem desired to chastise them? Therefore it was absolutely necessary to prostrate oneself before HaShem, and HaShem would do what is good in His eyes. And the approach of Baal Maasei HaShem is entirely half-baked and has neither salt nor spice, and it is beneath me to exert myself to reject things that are self-evidently rejected — with apologies for his honor." "<b>And I too</b> have a resting-place in the words of the early ones, of blessed memory, and I found in the Midrash (Yalkut Shimoni, Chukat) the following in their language: ״יַעַן לֹא הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם״ etc. — four sins are written here: you did not believe, you did not sanctify, you acted treacherously, you rebelled. ״You did not believe״ — I did not tell you to strike, and you struck. ״You did not sanctify״ — to bring forth water from any rock you wish. ״You acted treacherously״ — that you said ״from this rock.״ ״You rebelled״ — that I said to you ״וְדִבַּרְתֶּם אֶל הַסֶּלַע,״ repeat one chapter over it, and you transgressed My word. Thus far is their language." "And on the basis of this Midrash we will take our stand on the explanation of the holy words in the statement <b>Take the staff and speak to the rock, etc., and you will bring forth for them water from the rock</b>. And there are several difficulties to raise: (1) Why would HaShem command to take the staff, since the bringing forth of water is by speech alone? (2) Why did He repeat and say <b>and you will bring forth for them water</b> after He already said ״and it will give its waters״? (3) It was necessary to say a second time <b>from the rock</b>, and it was not sufficient with its being mentioned just above." "Behold, according to the midrash the intent of the verse is revealed to us retroactively as correct, for what it says — <b>Take et the staff</b> — is for the elevation of the King, and an indication that supreme power was in his hand, and not for striking. And He was not concerned for the error that it was for striking, from what He explicitly said ״וְדִבַּרְתֶּם״, and Moses did not do so but struck, and this is the first sin that the author of the midrash mentioned. And the statement <b>and you shall speak to the rock</b> is that he should recite one chapter over it, and he did not do so — and this is the second sin. And the statement <b>and you shall bring forth for them water from the rock</b> — the intention in this statement is to say: even from another rock, when they wish to bring forth for them from it, he should bring forth for them from whichever rock they desire. And therefore too He mentioned the rock a second time and did not attach it to the first mention, because that is not what He referred to. And he did not do so, and the proof is from what he said to them: ״שִׁמְעוּ נָא הַמּוֹרִים״ — this tells that they were rebelling about something, and from what he finished saying <b>הֲמִן הַסֶּלַע</b> etc., it tells that they were rebelling about the rock, saying: from this rock — whereas he said ״הֲמִן הַסֶּלַע הַזֶּה״ etc., meaning that this rock is not fit to bring forth water. Thus there were in Moses' hand two other errors: (1) HaShem said: bring forth for them from whichever rock they wish, and he did not do so; (2) he made the possibility absolute for them, as he said ״הֲמִן הַסֶּלַע״ etc. And these are the second and third sins that the author of the midrash counted." "<b>And it devolves</b> upon us, as an obligation of man, to give a reason for the man of Elohim regarding the four errors, of which he is not fitting for even one of them." "<b>And it appears</b> that Moses understood the statement of HaShem with wisdom, but on account of the intensity of his fear of HaShem he erred, and this came about as follows. What HaShem said to him, ״קַח אֶת הַמַּטֶּה״, he understood in it two ways — (1) for striking and (2) for elevation and for supreme power in his hand, since through the medium of the staff, upon which the Explicit Name is inscribed, he would perform a wonder. And the truth is that the plain sense of the words more properly supports striking with it, for the second interpretation is somewhat far-fetched. Even though we find that HaShem said to him (Exodus 4:17) ״וְאֶת הַמַּטֶּה הַזֶּה תִּקַּח בְּיָדֶךָ אֲשֶׁר״ etc. — there he performed actions with the staff. And the recollection of striking came from the statement of HaShem nearby: ״וְהוֹצֵאתָ לָהֶם מַיִם מִן הַסֶּלַע״, which should not have needed to be said after He already said ״וְדִבַּרְתֶּם״ etc. ״וְנָתַן מֵימָיו״, as we examined above. And Moses understood that the intent of HaShem was to tell him that speech alone would not suffice, but rather the power of the action he would perform through it would bring forth water. And in this, its end revealed its beginning — that the taking of the staff is to perform an action with it to bring forth the water from the rock. And even though the interpretation that HaShem intended arose in Moses' mind, as we said — that it came to say he should bring forth from whichever rock they desire — he inclined to interpret this interpretation in light of what preceded it, the statement of taking the staff. It did not seem right in his eyes that taking alone without doing anything with it would suffice. And even though this is not a necessary inference, nearby I shall explain properly the reason for his inclination toward what he did. And the statement ״וְדִבַּרְתֶּם״ etc. — the plain sense of the matter is, besides the action of the staff, further that they must speak to it explicitly: ״bring forth your waters״ — and this is what Moses understood, and it did not arise in his mind that the statement ״וְדִבַּרְתֶּם״ means that they should recite one chapter over it." "<b>And the discerning one</b> with the eye of intellect will see that the change in the meaning of the statement <b>וְדִבַּרְתֶּם</b> changes according to the intent of the statement <b>קַח אֶת הַמַּטֶּה</b>, for reason dictates that the rock is not a living being that understands human speech when Moses speaks to it, but rather in one of two ways: either through the medium of striking with the staff upon which the Explicit Name is inscribed, it would arouse the life-giving force within it — as I explained elsewhere, that there is no plant that does not have a vital portion according to its constitution, and that [force] would be awakened by the striking of the staff upon which a supreme Name is inscribed, as Moses indeed understood; or through reciting over it one chapter of Torah, which is eternal life from a holy mouth, it would arouse the force within the inanimate, and from this it would be aroused to give water. Therefore, if we interpret the taking of the staff as being for striking, there is no need to recite a chapter — this is its arousal; and if we interpret the taking of the staff as not being for striking, we would need to interpret <b>וְדִבַּרְתֶּם</b> as reciting a chapter over it. And therefore, according to the supreme Mind, when He said ״קַח אֶת הַמַּטֶּה״, if His intent was taking alone, His intent in the statement <b>וְדִבַּרְתֶּם</b> was to recite one chapter — whereas Moses, who understood the taking of the staff as being for striking, interpreted <b>וְדִבַּרְתֶּם</b> according to its plain sense." "<b>And at all events,</b> neither of the two ways in HaShem's statement ״קַח אֶת הַמַּטֶּה״ were hidden from Moses' eyes. But the interpretation of ״וְדִבַּרְתֶּם״ as reciting a chapter over it — this is not the plain sense of the words, and this interpretation did not arise in Moses' mind. And he reasoned in his own mind as the prophet of HaShem and said: Behold, before me are two ways. And if I do speech alone without striking, I fear lest the true interpretation is to strike — and all the more so since it is more plain-sense, and furthermore it is a far-fetched thing that the inanimate would be aroused by the speech of a human being, as I wrote nearby. Therefore if I speak without striking, not in the manner that HaShem said, the rock will not yield its waters, and the name of Heaven will be profaned — a great profanation before the assembly before whom I perform the miracle — and I will be liable with my life to the King. Therefore I will choose rather to strike, and I will incline toward the interpretation that is more proper in the meaning of the statement ״קַח אֶת הַמַּטֶּה״, and the statement ״וְהוֹצֵאתָ לָהֶם מַיִם״ etc. will also be fulfilled, as I explained. And he did not do striking alone, but rather with the combination of speech — he struck and said to the rock: ״Give forth your waters״. And in this there is no concern lest the rock not yield its waters and the name of Heaven be profaned." "He also inclined to explain the intent of the phrase <b>וְהוֹצֵאתָ לָהֶם מַיִם</b> as referring to striking, even though it falls under the category of doubt whether the intent is a rock that would be willing, for his own reason — because he was apprehensive of the explanation that he understood it to mean striking, and how much the more so since he was more inclined as mentioned. And if so, when he would come to bring forth water from another rock, even by means of striking, it would not yield its waters — if we say that the intent of HaShem was only concerning the known rock, and through this the name of Heaven would be profaned, Heaven forbid. Therefore he chose to bring forth from the known rock, and as El Elyon precisely gauges in the phrase <b>וְנָתַן מֵימָיו</b>, for this justifies that it is said of the known rock that has waters in it — and it is Miriam's well that had withheld its waters. And although it says afterward ״וְהוֹצֵאתָ לָהֶם מַיִם מִן הַסֶּלַע״, the meaning is from the known rock mentioned just before, and it turns out that all of Moshe's intention was nothing but to be concerned for the honor of His name, may He be blessed, and the error resulted under constraint." "<b>And with this</b> we find a reason for all of Moshe's errors: the reason he struck — he was apprehensive about what is heard from the words of HaShem that striking is necessary, and if so without striking it would not yield its waters and the name of Heaven would be profaned. And that he did not repeat one paragraph — this is not the plain sense of ״וְדִבַּרְתֶּם״, and Moshe did not understand it so, since he understood that the staff was for striking, as I wrote above. And the reason he did not bring forth water from another rock — since it is not compelled by the meaning of the phrase ״וְהוֹצֵאתָ לָהֶם מַיִם״ that it speaks of another rock, there is grounds for concern that it would not yield, and there would be a great profanation of the Name. Also he reasoned: why perform a new miracle to bring forth water from another rock when there is no need for it? And it is a well-known matter that a miracle is not performed except under great necessity. And from now on there is no room to blame him for the statement ״הֲמִן הַסֶּלַע״ etc., since it was not definitive in the statement of HaShem that He told him so. Therefore when they came and said: bring forth for us from this rock, he said to them <b>שִׁמְעוּ נָא הַמּוֹרִים</b> — a term of contention and a term of instruction, for they were intending to test the power on high, whether He would bring forth from any rock or from this rock alone, and they were instructing him to bring forth from another rock, and Moshe was insistent about the matter and said etc." "<b>And let it not</b> be displeasing in your eyes to say that Moshe did not understand the true intent of HaShem, for prophecy does not come to the righteous through reasoning, and therefore you find that they said, of blessed memory (Bava Batra 12a): a sage is preferable to a prophet. And in what is before us, the meaning that HaShem intended was not hidden from the eyes of Moshe either, but a doubt fell to him regarding another possible meaning, and out of concern for the honor of the Blessed One, he inclined toward what he did as mentioned." "And now that we have found a reason for the deed of the prophet, I will lift my eyes to understand His statement, may He be blessed, in His insistence in the phrase <b>יַעַן לֹא הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם בִּי לְהַקְדִּישֵׁנִי</b> — meaning: you made the determination toward the side of the concern that the rock would not yield water unless it be the known rock and specifically by means of striking, and you did not determine toward the side of the holiness of My name, to sanctify Me by bringing forth water from whatever rock they themselves wished and without striking — for through this HaShem would have been sanctified in their eyes when they saw that even the inanimate, whatsoever it may be, performs wonders by speech alone and by mere mention, changing upon it one paragraph, and it was incumbent upon you to nullify the apprehension through the intensity of the faith that you were required to hold." "<b>And the depth</b> of the matter is that if Moshe and Aharon had performed a great sanctification of the Name in the immediacy of the moment, with the congregation also being entirely whole and righteous, they would have established the faith of HaShem complete in their hearts, and the portion of evil would have been banished from them, and they would have had the power to establish their faith for all generations. And from that point Moshe and Aharon would have entered the land, and Moshe would have built the Temple, and there would have been no concern about the casting of His wrath upon Israel when they sinned, as stated in their words (Midrash Tehillim 79) on the verse ״מִזְמוֹר לְאָסָף״ etc., and we have written of it in other places. And since the rectification of the matter came through them and they did not do it, the matter was sealed." "And HaShem said <b>לָכֵן</b> — meaning, because of this; it is also the language of an oath that they shall not bring them, etc., for HaShem foresaw what they were destined to do at the end of days in the land of Israel, as Moses himself said to them (Deuteronomy 31:29): ״יָדַעְתִּי כִּי הַשְׁחֵת תַּשְׁחִיתוּן״. And therefore, if Moses were to enter the land and build the Holy Temple, HaShem would need to cast His wrath upon them when they sin, and not upon the Temple, and all Israel would be consumed, heaven forbid. Therefore He said <b>לָכֵן לֹא תָבִיאוּ</b>, etc." "And Scripture was precise in saying <b>אֶת הַקָּהָל הַזֶּה</b>. They said in the Midrash (Yalkut Shimoni 764): ״this״ assembly you do not bring in, but you will bring in all of Israel in the time to come, as it is written ״וְעָנְתָה שָׁמָּה כִּימֵי נְעוּרֶיהָ״ (Hosea 2:17) — this refers to Moses and Aaron; end quote. And from the implication of the words we may hear that if they had sanctified the Name of Heaven before their eyes, that generation would have been like the generation in which the son of David comes, when HaShem will remove the spirit of impurity and the earth will be filled with the knowledge of HaShem." "<b>וְהִשְׁקִיתָ אֶת הָעֵדָה.</b> There is a fine point in Scripture's saying <b>וְהִשְׁקִיתָ</b> in the causative (hif'il) form. Perhaps it intends one of two explanations: either, because Israel were parched with thirst, HaShem commands to give them drink in measured portions so that they would not come to harm — for otherwise, if they were to drink as they pleased because of the abundance of their thirst, they would drink much and endanger themselves and die. Or it may mean that he should give them to drink as much as needed and not be concerned about harm to them, for those waters were miraculous in every detail, both in their coming into being and in their effect. And that is what it says <b>וְהִשְׁקִיתָ</b> — that even if they wish to drink to satiation, you shall give them to drink as much as needed." "And it was necessary to mention <b>עֵדָה וּבְעִירָם</b>, to put the congregation before the animals. Even though Scripture says ״וְנָתַתִּי עֵשֶׂב בְּשָׂדְךָ לִבְהֶמְתְּךָ וְאָכַלְתָּ״ etc., and they of blessed memory said (Berakhot 40a) that one must feed his animal first and afterward himself — all this is when there is no danger involved; but when there is a concern of danger, his own life takes precedence. And see what I explained in the portion of Rebekah (Genesis 24:19) regarding the giving of drink to Eliezer and his camels."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {1 "For items 3–5, the Hebrew reads הֱבִיאוֹ הַנִּזְכָּר (he, the aforementioned one, brought [the opinion]); resolved as referring back to ibn Ezra who cites other views. For item 10, בַּעַל מַעֲשֵׂה ה׳ is Abarbanel's epithet. The verse citation in item 8 is rendered (Psalms 106:33) per the normalization rule.", 2 "קִמְּשׂוֹנִים (kimshonim) is rendered 'half-baked' — literally related to kemach/meal-dough, i.e., insipid; paired with the following 'neither salt nor spice.' The wordplay קַלַּנִי מִזְּרוֹעִי ('lighter than my arm') is rendered idiomatically as 'beneath me.'", 3 "The citation תשס״ד appears to be a typographical or OCR corruption of חֻקַּת (Chukat), the relevant parashah; rendered accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:12:44.480526865Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}